WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.340
Film Courage: Can we talk about Chekhov’s
gun?

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:05.380
Adam Skelter, author/story artist/screenwriter:
So basically Chekhov’s gun is (the axiom

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:10.350
is) if there’s a gun on the wall, you have
to use it.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:16.000
If the gun doesn’t go off then you’ve
set up an expectation that you didn’t deliver

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.490
on.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:23.650
And it’s a good principle and again it’s
one of those principles where generally speaking

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.039
you want to obey it.

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:28.950
But again look at NO COUNTRY FOR OLD MEN.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:36.550
Llewellyn Moss is a loaded gun on the wall
and in the end he fails and we don’t get

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.600
what we expected but it meant something to
the story.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:48.190
It’s like every principle, it helps us to
understand that…the general principle is

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:54.250
that if you are going to talk about something,
no detail in any story should ever be wasted.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:59.170
Especially with screenwriting you want to
be as sparse as possible, you want to be as

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.240
specific as possible.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:06.549
So when you’re writing something it all
has to be…you should regard every single

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:14.009
word as a kind of set-up that you now have
a debt to resolve.

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:19.099
But that said if you are going to break that
rule, have a really good reason for it.

00:01:19.099 --> 00:01:23.300
NO COUNTRY FOR OLD MEN is the perfect reason
for it because it directly contributed to

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.779
the interpretation of the theme.

00:01:24.779 --> 00:01:30.419
Film Courage: So even if you just want to
paint (you know like in novels) will be much

00:01:30.419 --> 00:01:35.759
more apt to…an author is going to paint
a world and maybe add little details when

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:41.159
in a screenplay you’re saying don’t do
too much of that world because it’s a waste

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:42.159
of money.

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:43.840
Adam: I would say it even without the writing.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:44.840
Film Courage: Oh really?

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.869
Adam: Because it really comes down to the
voice the language.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:53.469
And a lot of times when you’re setting something
up, you’re trying to plant seeds of something

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:57.100
up you are planting seeds that you don’t
want the audience to pay attention to because

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.920
later it pays off and comes through and you’re
like “Oh, I didn’t even see that coming?”

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.090
But if you didn’t set it up beforehand it
feels like a cheat when suddenly introduced.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:10.079
It really just comes down to really knowing
the craft.

00:02:10.079 --> 00:02:15.180
It’s super specific to the needs of your
story and a lot of that will depend on get

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.950
it in front of other people, see if it feels
like a cheat, see if it feels like you’re

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.140
setting something up.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:25.319
Sometimes if you say the laser gun sitting
on the table you’re going to be like “The

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:26.319
laser gun?

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:27.989
Are we in the future?”

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:33.090
And those are things you need to pay attention
to and a lot of that is fine tooth combing

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:40.500
your script and getting other people to read
it and gauging their expectations, getting

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.510
as much feedback as you can.

